//! Dropping the store shuts down the runtime and closes the database in the
//! background, without waiting for either to finish. The database stays locked
//! until it is closed, so loading the store again right after dropping it will
//! usually fail. Also, some parts of the persistent state won't make it to
//! disk, so operations involving large partial blobs will have a large initial
//! delay on the next startup.
//!
//! It is also not guaranteed that all write operations will make it to disk.
//! The on-disk store will be in a consistent state, but might miss some writes
//! in the last seconds before shutdown.
//!
//! To avoid this, await [`crate::api::Store::shutdown`] before dropping the
//! store. It returns once the database is closed and ephemeral state is saved
//! to disk, so the store can be loaded again right away.

#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct Options {
    /// Path options.
    pub path: PathOptions,
    /// Inline storage options.
    pub inline: InlineOptions,
    /// Transaction batching options.
    pub batch: BatchOptions,
    /// Gc configuration.
    pub gc: Option<GcConfig>,
    /// What kind of crash the store stays consistent through.
    pub crash_consistency: CrashConsistency,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, Default, PartialEq, Eq)]
#[non_exhaustive]
pub enum CrashConsistency {
    /// Consistent after a process crash; recovers from an OS crash or power loss on the next open.
    ///
    /// No data files are synced, so an OS crash or power loss can leave entries
    /// that claim data that never reached the disk. The store records the boot
    /// id while it is open, and if it finds one from another boot session on
    /// open, every entry with files checks them again when it is next loaded.
    /// Until then it may report less data than it has, never more.
    ///
    /// A clean shutdown does not sync, so an OS crash within seconds after it,
    /// before the OS wrote out its cache, goes unnoticed. Where there is no boot
    /// id, every unclean shutdown counts as a possible OS crash.
    #[default]
    ProcessCrash,
    /// Consistent after an OS crash or power loss too, at the cost of fsyncs.
    ///
    /// Data files and the data directory are synced before the metadata that
    /// depends on them is recorded, for imports as well as downloads. That
    /// costs a few fsyncs for every blob above the inline threshold, which
    /// slows down imports and downloads of many small blobs considerably.
    /// On Linux, Android, macOS and iOS, opening the store also flushes the data
    /// filesystem after recovery, before recording the new run. This includes
    /// writes inherited from an earlier run, but may wait for unrelated writes
    /// on the same filesystem. If the flush fails, opening the store returns an
    /// error and leaves the previous run record in place for the next attempt.
    /// Other platforms do not yet perform this startup flush, so switching from
    /// `ProcessCrash` can leave earlier writes unsynced.
    PowerLoss,
}

#[derive(derive_more::Debug, Clone)]
pub struct GcConfig {
    /// Interval in which to run garbage collection.
    pub interval: Duration,
    /// Optional callback to manually add protected blobs.
    ///
    /// The callback is called before each garbage collection run. It gets a `&mut HashSet<Hash>`
    /// and returns a future that returns [`ProtectOutcome`]. All hashes that are added to the
    /// [`HashSet`] will be protected from garbage collection during this run.
    ///
    /// In normal operation, return [`ProtectOutcome::Continue`] from the callback. If you return
    /// [`ProtectOutcome::Abort`], the garbage collection run will be aborted.Use this if your
    /// source of hashes to protect returned an error, and thus garbage collection should be skipped
    /// completely to not unintentionally delete blobs that should be protected.
    #[debug("ProtectCallback")]
    pub add_protected: Option<ProtectCb>,
}

